{"id":"assisted_pullup","name":"Assisted pull-up","cue":"Drive your elbows down to your hips.","research":"Assisted chin / dip machine used as a pull-up: kneeling on a counterweighted pad, overhead pronated bar at about 1.65x biacromial width (grip x 0.19 H each side), the same pull as the lat pulldown (research.json biomech.lat_pulldown: shoulder elevation 165 -> 40, elbow 10 -> 135, scapulae elevated -> depressed first, via key M) with the bar fixed and the body rising instead (pad travel 0.38 H = 68 cm). Kneeling upright (thighs vertical, knees 90, shins on the pad, ankles plantar-flexed 35), trunk 5 -> 15 deg behind vertical as the elbows drive down and back. The hands are solved by IK to the bar (elbow ~5 at the dead hang, ~128 at the top, bar just in front of the upper chest); the arm angles are the pulldown keys, the pole sends the elbows out, down and back. Machine: two posts, a top beam with the grip bar on short struts, a carriage on the posts carrying the kneeling pad, a cable over the beam to a stack in front of the tower that sinks as the pad rises (a counterweight: the stack carries the assistance). Camera from behind as the pulldown (the lats face the camera). Secondary muscles are not drawn: the biceps are hidden from behind and the upper back fuses with the lats (as in the pulldown).","targets":{"tol":10,"note":"Shoulder angles use the world-frame read-back (_w) as in the lat pulldown; the plane is not checked above 150 deg of elevation.","A":{"trunk_flexion":-5,"hip_flexion":-4,"knee_flexion":90,"shoulder_elevation_w":157,"elbow_flexion":6},"B":{"trunk_flexion":-15,"hip_flexion":-11,"knee_flexion":90,"shoulder_elevation_w":46,"shoulder_plane_w":-24,"elbow_flexion":130}},"muscles":{"primary":["lat"],"secondary":[],"side":"both"},"camera":{"az":150,"el":12,"dAz":0,"dEl":0,"fill":0.58,"note":"Rear view as the lat pulldown (az 150, el 12): the lats face the camera. The stack stands in front of the tower, behind the figure from this view, and the base i